<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · other</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prohibition in the United States</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1920–1933</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prohibition in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> was the nationwide ban on the production, import and sale of alcoholic drinks from 1920 to 1933, under the Eighteenth Amendment to the Constitution. It encouraged bootlegging, smuggling and organised crime, with rum runners operating from the Bahamas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. It was repealed by the Twenty-first Amendment in December 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prohibition-in-the-united-states.jpg" alt="Prohibition in the United States" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author or not provided · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detroit_police_inspecting_equipment_found_in_a_clandestine_underground_brewery_during_the_prohibition_era_-_NARA_-_541928.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
